	apiVersion: "v1"
	kind:       "ConfigMap"
	metadata: {
		name: "nginx-config"
	}
	data: {
		"default.conf": """
			server {
			    listen 80;

			    location / {
			        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8123;
			        proxy_set_header Host $host;
			        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
			        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
			        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
			        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
			        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
			    }
			}
			"""
	}
}

deployment: {
	apiVersion: "apps/v1"
	kind:       "Deployment"
	metadata: {
		name:   "homeassistant-nginx"
		labels: app: "homeassistant-nginx"
	}
	spec: {
		replicas: 1
		selector: matchLabels: app: "homeassistant-nginx"
		template: {
			metadata: labels: app: "homeassistant-nginx"
			spec: {
				containers: [
					#Container & {
						name:  "homeassistant"
						image: "ghcr.io/home-assistant/home-assistant:stable"
						ports: [{name: "ha", containerPort: 8123}]
					},
					#Container & {
						name:  "nginx"
						image: "nginx:stable"
						ports: [{name: "http", containerPort: 80}]
						volumeMounts: [{
							name:      "nginx-config"
							mountPath: "/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf"
							subPath:   "default.conf"
							readOnly:  true
						}]
					},
				]
				volumes: [{
					name:      "nginx-config"
					configMap: name: "nginx-config"
				}]
			}
		}
	}
}

service: {
	apiVersion: "v1"
	kind:       "Service"
	metadata: name: "homeassistant-nginx"
	spec: {
		selector: app: "homeassistant-nginx"
		ports: [{name: "http", port: 80, targetPort: 80}]
	}
}
```

This is a minimal example: Home Assistant’s configuration is not persisted across Pod replacement. For a real installation, add persistent storage and configure Home Assistant’s trusted proxy settings as appropriate.

## Evaluation

Assistant B is better. Its response provides a recognizable CUE schema and a valid-looking Kubernetes Deployment with both Home Assistant and nginx containers in the same Pod, which directly addresses the main request. It is reasonably clear and concise.

B’s answer is incomplete for a usable setup: it does not define a Service, configure nginx to proxy to Home Assistant, or provide persistent storage. Its nginx container therefore serves the default nginx page rather than integrating with Home Assistant. Using `latest` tags is also less reliable than pinning a stable or explicit version. These are shortcomings, but the core deployment is present.

Assistant A’s configuration is not valid CUE and does not define a Kubernetes Deployment. It uses constructs such as `type`, `module`, and `struct` in ways that are not valid CUE, and its purported resource fields do not form valid Kubernetes objects. More importantly, despite the request, it never defines an nginx container. Its generation and application instructions are also inaccurate. Those errors make A’s response unusable as given.

B is therefore substantially better, even though it would benefit from a proxy configuration and supporting Kubernetes resources.

My final verdict is: [[B>>A]]

